  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nSfc boundary works into the E UP through the day today, with weak \nlow pressure system across northern lower MI. Sfc wind pattern today \nwill be northeast across northeast lower and eastern upper, and \nsouthwest across much of northern lower. Weak pressure gradient and \nwinds aloft though so generally light winds. Main area of low \npressure associated with a short wave aloft will be moving northeast \nthrough the day across the far east Dakotas and MN. Few showers \ntoday will largely be confined to eastern upper Michigan, closer to \nthe Canadian border, subsequent low level convergence, and the \nbetter warm/moisture advection. Low end instability through the day \nas well, and thus, there could be a rumble of thunder/convection out \nthere, but some of the latest CAMs say it stays to the north across \nthe Canadian Shield. Still worth watching through the day as other \npieces of guidance do drop scattered showers, and a rumble of \nthunder, across the E UP. Either way, not a huge deal and \nprecipitation maker today. Temperatures will be quite warm again \ntoday south of the bridge. Potential for 70s and low 80s, within a \nfew degrees of records but not expected to eclipse.  \n\nMid level short wave and subsequent low pressure system move \neastward tonight, with the low pressure tracking right across \nnorthern lower Michigan. Consequently, swath of rain and showers are \nanticipated tonight into Tuesday morning. Showers will linger on \nTuesday due to the low level convergence. All that being said, the \nbest chance for steadier rainfall should be to the north of the low \npressure system and just east of the main vort max which is progged \nto be ~Tip of the Mitt and northward. There will be a convective \nelement to this precipitation as well, especially across northern \nlower MI where the warmest temperatures and highest theta e pools. \nThis could even out the rainfall distribution to some extent, or \nspread some of the rains farther south. Some areas have the \npotential for a quarter to half an inch, maybe more in the \nsynoptically favored areas. Cool advection behind this system will \nbring the lovely 70s to an end, with high temps on Wednesday in the \nmid 50s to mid 60s.\n\nTowards the middle to latter portions of this week, significant \nridging builds across the West Coast/Intermountain West, with energy \nriding the eastern periphery of that ridge. This will bring a \nsignificant and fast moving upper trough across the central portions \nof Canada, diving down into northern Michigan. Sfc response will be \nfor a low pressure system to move across the Great Lakes region as \nwell. Lift and convergence from these features will result in rain \nshowers, perhaps steadier periods of rain, across northern MI later \nThursday into Friday. Upper low begins to deepen to our east, and as \nsuch, so will the sfc response. With a stout 1040mb high pressure \nfilling in behind this deepening low pressure, windy conditions are \nanticipated on Friday. The last component to this system will be the \ncold advection, as you'd expect from a fast moving, pretty deep \nupper low moving down from western and central portions of Canada. \nHigh temps will be into the low 50s at least Friday and Saturday, \ndropping below freezing overnight. Looks like a relatively quick \nrecovery temperature wise though as heights rise by the end of next \nweekend.",
